"""Show the spatial-QVF architecture and verified direct-fitting comparison."""

import argparse
import base64
import html
import json
from pathlib import Path


TEMPLATE = r'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>A spatial decoder for QVF</title><style>
:root{font-family:var(--font-sans,system-ui);color:var(--foreground);color-scheme:light dark}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0}p{line-height:1.55;margin:10px 0 14px}h2{font-size:20px;margin:0 0 12px}a{color:var(--primary)}.panel{padding:16px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);background:var(--card);margin:18px 0}.muted,figcaption{font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--muted-foreground)}.chart{height:240px;width:100%}.chart svg{width:100%;height:100%;display:block}.pictures{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:10px}figure{margin:0}canvas{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;image-rendering:pixelated;border-radius:4px}figcaption{margin-top:7px}label{display:block;margin:12px 0 6px;font-size:14px}select{width:100%;padding:8px;font:inherit;color:var(--foreground);background:var(--background);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px}input{width:100%;accent-color:var(--primary)}.stats{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.legend{display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;font-size:13px}.legend span:before{content:'';display:inline-block;width:13px;height:3px;background:var(--chart-1);margin-right:5px;vertical-align:middle}.legend span:nth-child(2):before{background:var(--chart-2)}.legend span:nth-child(3):before{background:var(--chart-3)}.flow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 18px 1.5fr 18px 1.2fr 18px 1.3fr;align-items:stretch;gap:3px;margin:15px 0}.node{padding:12px 8px;border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;text-align:center;font-size:13px;line-height:1.4}.node strong{display:block;margin-bottom:6px}.node.spatial{border-color:var(--chart-3)}.node.quantum{border-color:var(--chart-2)}.arrow{align-self:center;text-align:center;color:var(--muted-foreground)}.table-wrap{overflow-x:auto}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}th,td{padding:8px 5px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);text-align:right}th:first-child,td:first-child{text-align:left}@media(max-width:520px){h2{font-size:18px}.pictures{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:14px 10px}.flow{grid-template-columns:1fr}.arrow{transform:rotate(90deg);height:17px}.node{padding:9px}.node strong{margin:0 0 3px}}
</style></head><body>__ASSETS__
<p><strong>We tested a spatial decoder for QVF while retaining its original five-qubit quantum head.</strong> This is a first test of a more useful image architecture: the same 4,744 training images, directly fitted 64-number codes, three seeds and 50,000 updates per run.</p>
<p>The original QVF already learns image structure through coordinates, a shared code and shared network weights. Convolutions add an explicit way to reuse neighbourhood patterns. The paper's main shared image experiment fits 50 images; its shape collection contains three shapes. The original version also describes a four-image higher-resolution experiment. These are small collections, not exclusively low-resolution tests. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2508.10900v2#S5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">QVF v2 evaluation</a>; <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2508.10900v1#S5.SS2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">original v1 image experiments</a>.</p>
<div class="panel"><h2>Where the convolutions go</h2><p>Previously, every pixel query received the same global code plus its coordinates. The new decoder first expands that code into a spatial grid of learned features:</p>
<div class="flow" role="img" aria-label="64-number code generates convolutional feature grids; local features and a coordinate enter an amplitude network, then the original five-qubit circuit produces RGB">
<div class="node"><strong>Image code</strong>64 numbers<br>One code per image</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="node spatial"><strong>Convolutional features</strong>4 × 4 → 8 × 8 → 16 × 16<br>Learn neighbourhood patterns</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="node"><strong>Pixel query</strong>Local features + coordinates<br>32 quantum amplitudes</div><div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="node quantum"><strong>Original QVF head</strong>5 qubits, depth 5<br>Measurements → RGB</div></div>
<p>These grids are feature maps, not little RGB photographs. Convolutional filters combine nearby features and reuse the same learned operation at many locations. The entire grid comes from the 64-number code: the decoder gets no extra copy of the original image.</p>
<p class="muted">Local features with a coordinate decoder have a classical precedent in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.09161" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LIIF</a>. Here we generate features from a code and retain QVF's amplitude map, circuit and direct measurement readout. This is an architectural extension, not vanilla QVF or a claim of new classical convolution machinery.</p></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Does the spatial design help at similar capacity?</h2><p>The main control is a wider pointwise QVF with almost the same number of parameters. That comparison helps separate the spatial design from simply giving the decoder more capacity.</p>
<label for="metric">Measurement on all 4,744 fitted images</label><select id="metric"><option value="psnr_db">Reconstruction fidelity — PSNR, higher is better</option><option value="gradient_mse">Adjacent-pixel error — lower is better</option></select>
<div id="summary-chart" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Training reconstruction comparison between original, parameter-matched pointwise, and convolutional-feature QVF"></div>
<p id="summary" class="stats"></p><p id="edge" class="muted stats"></p><p class="muted">Dots show means over three seeds; whiskers show one sample standard deviation, not a confidence interval. Every score uses raw, unclipped output at the final 50,000 updates. Parameter counts exclude the identical 303,616 fitted image-code entries.</p></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>Inspect the same image through all three decoders</h2><label for="seed">Training seed</label><select id="seed"><option value="0">Seed 0</option><option value="1">Seed 1</option><option value="2">Seed 2</option></select><label for="photo">Training image <output id="photo-number">1</output> of 256 in the fixed gallery</label><input id="photo" type="range" min="0" max="255" value="0" step="1">
<div class="pictures"><figure><canvas id="original" width="32" height="32" aria-label="Original image"></canvas><figcaption>Original image</figcaption></figure><figure><canvas id="baseline128" width="32" height="32" aria-label="Original pointwise QVF reconstruction"></canvas><figcaption>Original QVF<br>48,950 parameters</figcaption></figure><figure><canvas id="matched50k" width="32" height="32" aria-label="Parameter-matched pointwise QVF reconstruction"></canvas><figcaption>Wider pointwise QVF<br>299,705 parameters</figcaption></figure><figure><canvas id="conv50k" width="32" height="32" aria-label="Convolutional feature QVF reconstruction"></canvas><figcaption>Spatial QVF<br>299,894 parameters</figcaption></figure></div>
<p id="image-stats" class="muted stats"></p><p class="muted">The 256 gallery positions were fixed before training. All 4,744 reconstructions are saved and included in the scores. Display enlargement uses no smoothing; RGB clipping is for display only.</p></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>How the fits developed during training</h2><div id="learning" class="chart" role="img" aria-label="Full-training-set PSNR over optimizer updates for the selected seed"></div><div class="legend"><span>Original QVF</span><span>Wider pointwise QVF</span><span>Spatial QVF</span></div><p id="curve-note" class="muted stats"></p><p class="muted">These are full-training-set evaluations from 5,000 to 50,000 updates, not sampled minibatch scores. Each image participates in a sampled training batch about 337 times. Identical seeds use identical initial codes, initial quantum angles, image order and queried pixels. Classical weights are fresh in each architecture.</p></div>
<div class="panel"><h2>What remains quantum, and what remains untested?</h2><p><strong>Every RGB output still passes through the original quantum circuit and measurement rule.</strong> The circuit trains jointly with the classical layers. There is no classical RGB bypass or extra image decoder after measurement. Adding spatial features does not require more qubits because the amplitude network still outputs 32 amplitudes per query.</p>
<p>This experiment uses exact classical simulation and measurement expectations, so quantum shot noise is not causing blur here. Circuit participation does not show that quantum computation improves the result: a separately trained circuit-removal or replacement control is needed for that question.</p>
<p><strong>This is a decoder fitting test.</strong> It tests whether the architecture can represent the training images more effectively. It does not yet test an encoder on new images, VAE regularization, diffusion generation, more image classes or higher resolutions.</p>
<p>A feature grid can be computed once and reused across pixel queries. The quantum part still needs a query per coordinate, with state-preparation and repeated measurement costs on hardware. Keeping five qubits therefore does not establish fast large-image rendering or hardware scalability.</p>
<p id="runtime" class="muted stats"></p><p class="muted">Both new models use the same direct-code objective: pixel MSE plus the original code-norm penalty. Learning rates, batch size, pixel sampling and optimizer updates are fixed. The comparison changes feature locality, activations and allocation of parameters together; it does not isolate a single convolution operation.</p></div>
<p id="verification" class="muted"></p>
<script>
const data=__DATA__,$=id=>document.getElementById(id),kinds=['baseline128','matched50k','conv50k'],labels=['Original QVF','Wider pointwise','Spatial QVF'],seed=$('seed'),photo=$('photo'),fmt=x=>x.toFixed(2);
function axes(el,min,max,digits=1){const w=el.clientWidth,h=240,L=digits>2?66:44,R=24,T=32,B=46,sy=v=>h-B-(v-min)/(max-min)*(h-T-B);let p='';for(let i=0;i<=4;i++){const v=min+(max-min)*i/4;p+=`<line x1="${L}" x2="${w-R}" y1="${sy(v)}" y2="${sy(v)}" stroke="var(--border)"/><text x="${L-7}" y="${sy(v)+4}" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--foreground)" font-size="11">${v.toFixed(digits)}</text>`}return{w,h,L,R,T,B,sy,p}}
function show(el,a,p){el.innerHTML=`<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 ${a.w} ${a.h}">${a.p}${p}</svg>`}
function summaryChart(){const metric=$('metric').value,vals=kinds.map(k=>data.summary[k][metric]),psnr=metric==='psnr_db',lo=Math.min(...vals.map(v=>v.mean-(v.sample_sd||0))),hi=Math.max(...vals.map(v=>v.mean+(v.sample_sd||0))),pad=Math.max((hi-lo)*.25,psnr?.25:.00005),a=axes($('summary-chart'),Math.max(0,lo-pad),hi+pad,psnr?1:4);let p='';vals.forEach((v,i)=>{const x=a.L+(i+.5)/3*(a.w-a.L-a.R),bottom=a.sy(v.mean-(v.sample_sd||0)),top=a.sy(v.mean+(v.sample_sd||0)),c=`var(--chart-${i+1})`;p+=`<path d="M${x},${bottom}V${top}M${x-5},${bottom}h10M${x-5},${top}h10" stroke="${c}" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="${x}" cy="${a.sy(v.mean)}" r="5" fill="${c}"/><text x="${x}" y="${top-10}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--foreground)" font-size="12">${v.mean.toFixed(psnr?2:5)}</text><text x="${x}" y="${a.h-25}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--foreground)" font-size="11">${['Original','Pointwise','Spatial'][i]}</text><text x="${x}" y="${a.h-10}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted-foreground)" font-size="10">${i?'300k params':'49k params'}</text>`});show($('summary-chart'),a,p);const delta=data.paired_changes.conv_minus_matched_psnr_db.mean;$('summary').textContent=`Final training PSNR: ${kinds.map((k,i)=>`${labels[i]} ${fmt(data.summary[k].psnr_db.mean)} ± ${fmt(data.summary[k].psnr_db.sample_sd)} dB`).join('; ')}. Spatial minus matched pointwise: ${delta>=0?'+':''}${fmt(delta)} dB.`;const edgeChange=100*(1-data.summary.conv50k.gradient_mse.mean/data.summary.matched50k.gradient_mse.mean);$('edge').textContent=`Spatial QVF's adjacent-pixel error is ${Math.abs(edgeChange).toFixed(1)}% ${edgeChange>=0?'lower':'higher'} than the matched pointwise model. This measures agreement in neighbouring pixel differences; it is not a complete measure of perceived sharpness.`}
function pictures(){const s=+seed.value,i=+photo.value,asset=data.images.find(r=>r.seed===s),run=data.runs.find(r=>r.seed===s);['original',...kinds].forEach((id,j)=>{const ctx=$(id).getContext('2d');ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled=false;ctx.drawImage(asset.asset,1+(i%16)*33,1+(Math.floor(i/16)+j*16)*33,32,32,0,0,32,32)});$('photo-number').textContent=i+1;$('image-stats').textContent=`Dataset image ${asset.dataset_indices[i]}; training-list position ${asset.training_positions[i]+1}. ${kinds.map((k,j)=>`${labels[j]}: ${fmt(run[k].gallery_psnr_db[i])} dB`).join(' · ')}.`}
function learning(){const run=data.runs.find(r=>r.seed===+seed.value),curves=kinds.map(k=>run[k].curve.filter(r=>r[0]>=5000)),values=curves.flat().map(r=>r[1]),a=axes($('learning'),Math.floor(Math.min(...values)-.2),Math.ceil(Math.max(...values)+.2)),sx=v=>a.L+(v-5000)/45000*(a.w-a.L-a.R);let p='';curves.forEach((curve,i)=>{p+=`<path d="${curve.map((r,j)=>`${j?'L':'M'}${sx(r[0])},${a.sy(r[1])}`).join(' ')}" fill="none" stroke="var(--chart-${i+1})" stroke-width="2"/>`});[5000,25000,50000].forEach(v=>p+=`<text x="${sx(v)}" y="${a.h-14}" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--foreground)" font-size="11">${v.toLocaleString()}</text>`);show($('learning'),a,p);$('curve-note').textContent=`Seed ${run.seed}. Lowest recorded fitting MSE occurred at: ${kinds.map((k,i)=>`${labels[i]}, step ${run[k].best_fitting_step.toLocaleString()}`).join('; ')}. A fixed update budget does not establish convergence.`}
data.images.forEach((r,i)=>r.asset=$(`asset-${i}`));summaryChart();learning();const time=k=>data.runs.map(r=>r[k].elapsed_seconds/60);$('runtime').textContent=`Observed training time on shared GPU 0: spatial ${Math.min(...time('conv50k')).toFixed(1)}–${Math.max(...time('conv50k')).toFixed(1)} minutes per seed; matched pointwise ${Math.min(...time('matched50k')).toFixed(1)}–${Math.max(...time('matched50k')).toFixed(1)} minutes. These are not isolated speed measurements. Matching parameters and updates does not match compute.`;$('verification').textContent=`Verified ${data.verification.new_runs} new runs and ${data.verification.baseline_runs} preserved reference runs. All training images were rendered again from their final checkpoints, scores recomputed, data splits and training budgets checked, and initial codes/circuit angles compared exactly. Original QVF source and baseline checkpoints are unchanged.`;
function activate(){if(window.qvfSpatialReady)return;seed.addEventListener('change',()=>{pictures();learning()});photo.addEventListener('input',pictures);$('metric').addEventListener('change',summaryChart);pictures();window.qvfSpatialReady=true;let width=0;new ResizeObserver(()=>{if(width!==document.body.clientWidth){width=document.body.clientWidth;summaryChart();learning()}}).observe(document.body)}
if(data.images.every(r=>r.asset.complete&&r.asset.naturalWidth>0)){activate()}else{window.addEventListener('load',activate,{once:true})}
</script></body></html>'''


def build(data, local=False):
    assets = []
    for i, item in enumerate(data["images"]):
        path = Path(item["path"])
        source = str(path) if local else "data:image/webp;base64," + base64.b64encode(path.read_bytes()).decode()
        assets.append(f'<img id="asset-{i}" src="{html.escape(source, quote=True)}" alt="">')
    display = {key: value for key, value in data.items() if key != "protocol"}
    display["images"] = [{key: value for key, value in item.items() if key != "path"} for item in data["images"]]
    return TEMPLATE.replace("__ASSETS__", '<div hidden>' + ''.join(assets) + '</div>').replace(
        "__DATA__", json.dumps(display, allow_nan=False).replace("</", "<\\/"))


def main():
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
    parser.add_argument("--data", type=Path, required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--output-stem", type=Path, required=True)
    args = parser.parse_args()
    paths = [Path(str(args.output_stem) + suffix) for suffix in (".html", "_local.html")]
    if any(p.exists() for p in paths):
        raise FileExistsError("Choose a fresh report output stem")
    data = json.loads(args.data.read_text())
    for path, local in zip(paths, (False, True)):
        with path.open("x") as handle:
            handle.write(build(data, local))
        print(path.resolve())


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
